# Disk cleanup

The command, or an AtE naming these records, approves the records this skill
writes (it writes none); removing anything needs AtE (rules file). Any
holder; `<name>` is your holder name. Other skills (`ptt-done`,
`ptt-pr-review`, `ptt-pr-cleanup`, `ptt-leave`) follow these steps too.

The user's direction comes first: when the user names run records or a
scratchpad to clean up, do it, whatever their `by:`, and use
`test-run gc PATH...` to inventory them. The rules below limit only what a
session proposes by itself.

## Inventory (read-only)

1. Run records: `<records>/bin/test-run gc --scratch <scratchpad>` (add the
   paths the user named; `--as <name>` when this session is not bound). It
   shows every run with its head, citations, and what may go, and gives
   commands only for runs whose `by:` is you: a run whose head (request or
   task) is open stays whole; once it is concluded, a run cited nowhere goes
   whole and a cited one only its build directories. Runs of others, runs
   without `by:`, and runs with no head are for information: report them;
   the user decides who handles them.
2. Scratchpad: only this session's own. Claude Code names it in the system
   prompt (the scratchpad directory); in Codex, state what you find (a
   temporary directory this session made), or that it is Unknown. Never
   anything outside it. From `--scratch`'s list, propose only what you no
   longer need; say what you keep and why.
3. The integrator also runs `<records>/bin/pr gc` and reports its table; its
   removals follow `/ptt-pr-cleanup`, which writes the cleanup messages.
4. Disk use: `df -h <workspace root> <scratchpad>`.
5. Report the tables, the disk use, and the commands exactly as printed.
   With more than about 20 commands, show their count, the total they free,
   and the first five, and ask whether to expand them all. End with the
   decision list right after its question line (rules file):

   ```
   Remove what is listed:
   1. Run all listed commands (recommended)
   2. Run only the ones I name
   3. Show all commands first
   4. No decision, discuss further
   ```

## Removal (on AtE)

6. Run the inventory again with the same arguments; if an approved command
   is gone or changed, report and stop.
7. Run the approved commands exactly as printed, one at a time; never edit,
   combine, or glob them. If one fails, stop and report.
8. Run the inventory again and `df -h`; report what was freed and what
   remains, and why.
